From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] phy: allwinner: A733 combo-PHY -- DP link rate 2700 Mb/s PMA table

COMB0's DisplayPort personality needs a different PMA configuration from
the SuperSpeed one `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` landed, and this is the first piece of it: the
rate table for 2700 Mb/s (HBR), the lane-count write, and a
phy_ops.configure to reach them. The USB3 path is not touched -- not one
existing line of table or code changes, and nothing in this tree calls
phy_configure() (dwc3 uses init/power_on/set_mode only).

Where the table comes from, and where it does not

The sequence is combo0_dp_set_rate() case 2700 of the vendor driver
(bsp/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c:1158-1385), taken on the
usb_dp_state == 0 branch -- Type-C pin assignment C/E, four DP lanes, no
concurrent SuperSpeed. 164 PMA writes plus the two wrapper writes the
vendor performs first (LINK_CTRL2 IDDQ clear at :1159-1161, LINK_CTRL1 =
0x01100001 at :1163). One of those 164 is the ssc_en == true arm of an
if/else, dropped, so the table ships 163 writes over 156 unique indices.

Per the tree's rule that vendor source is a hint and never evidence, that
sequence was then checked against silicon, with a capture taken on
the 8 GiB board on the vendor 6.6 stack with a live 4-lane DP-alt link at 2.7
Gbps (1920x1200, orientation REVERSE, pin assignment C, HPD asserted). Pin
assignment C is exactly usb_dp_state == 0, so the capture is a witness of
this branch and not of a neighbouring one. Per unique index:

	silicon reads back what the table writes : 71
	silicon differs                          :  4
	silicon cannot say                       : 81

The 4 that differ are TX_TXCC_MGNFS_MULT_000 on all four lanes, and they
are not a wrong value: link training owns that register.
combo0_dp_set_sw_pre() rewrites it at every swing/pre-emphasis step, and
the live quartet 0x0010/0x000E/0x000E/0x0010 is verbatim the vendor's own
dp_lv_table_2g7[sw=2][pre=0] MGNFS row (:556-559). At that same row TXDRV
is 0x0003 and CPOST is 0x0000, which is why the other three training-owned registers
agree -- training landed on the config value. All 16 training-owned
registers are marked "t" in the table; this table is not their authority,
and 8 of the 71 agreements are therefore not independent evidence (63
are).

The 81 the silicon cannot settle, and why each

	49  odd index, never sampled
	28  written zero, reads zero
	 4  written non-zero, reads zero

The 49 are the load-bearing number and they are a limit of the capture
method, not of the part. An odd index sits at byte offset 2 mod 4, and
both captures were taken with 32-bit reads on a 4-byte stride, so those
addresses were never read. Nor can the odd half be recovered from the even
read: a 32-bit read of this PMA returns the 16-bit register mirrored into
both halves rather than a packed pair. CMN_PLL0_INTDIV_M0 (0x0090) =
0x0180 and CMN_PLL0_FRACDIVL_M0 (0x0091) = 0x9DBA read back as 0x01800180,
not 0x9DBA0180; 0xC010 = 0x51D9 with 0xC011 = 0x0100 reads 0x51D951D9.
Zero of 537 non-zero DP words and zero of the non-zero words in the 6 GiB board's
dump have differing halves. A 16-bit read at 2-byte stride would settle all 49 on
the next board session -- that is the one measurement this patch is
missing and can name.

That has a consequence for `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`'s own header, which is worth stating
rather than quietly repairing: the four registers it calls
hardware-rewritten (0x01C1, 0xC011, 0xE003, 0xE005) are all odd indices,
so its "silicon reads 0x001B" for 0xE003 is the mirrored value of the even
neighbour 0xE002, not a reading of 0xE003. The conclusion `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` drew from
them may still be right -- 0xE003 is PHY_PMA_PLL_RAW_CTRL and 0x01C1 is
the PLL1 clock select that a rate change rewrites -- but it does not rest
on that reading. Here all four are carried as the vendor writes them and
marked unproven, per the same rule.

The 4 written non-zero and reading zero are RX_SDCAL0/1_INIT_TMR on RX
lanes 0 and 3. They read zero on both boards and in both personalities,
while lanes 1 and 2 hold 0x001A in both; lanes 0 and 3 are the pairs the
receptacle wires to SSTX. The likely reading
is that the RX CDB is not populated there. Unproven either way, and the
writes stay, because that is what the linked board ran.

Named registers, not invented ones

151 of the 156 indices carry a register name, and it is mainline's, not
ours: drivers/phy/cadence/phy-cadence-torrent.c places the common CDB at
0x0000, the TX lane CDB at 0x4000 + ln*0x200, the RX lane CDB at 0x8000 +
ln*0x200, PHY_PCS common at 0xC000 and PHY_PMA common at 0xE000, and two
of its three upstream variants use block_offset_shift = 0 with
reg_offset_shift = 1 -- byte offset = index << 1, the exact mapping `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`
validated here on silicon. So the decode now has an upstream witness as
well as a silicon one. The five indices with no upstream name (0x004B,
0xC010, 0xC011, 0xE005, 0xE006) are left unnamed; the UM does not name
them either.

Orientation, and why a REVERSE capture is admissible

The table is written for no mirror although the capture is REVERSE. That
is sound, not lucky: every lane-block entry is written to all four lanes
with the same value, so `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`'s ^0x600 mirror maps the table onto itself.
Checked mechanically -- of 87 lane indices the mirror moves exactly one
pair, RX_PSC_A3 lane 0 <-> lane 3, and only because of a vendor typo
(below).

Three vendor copy-paste faults, reproduced and named

Repairing them would be an unwitnessed deviation from the only sequence a
2.7 Gbps link has been observed to train on, so they are reproduced:
  - the group commented TX_PSC_A3 writes TX_PSC_A2 a second time; TX_PSC_A3
    (0x4103 + ln*0x200) is never written;
  - in the RX zero block, RX_PSC_A0 lane 0 is written twice and RX_PSC_A2
    lane 0 lands in the A3 group, so RX_PSC_A3 lane 0 (0x8003) is never
    written -- every value there is zero, so this is inert unless the reset
    default is not;
  - CMN_PLL1_SS_CTRL2_M0 (0x00D9) is written twice, 0x04C4 then 0x045F,
    the second time from inside the PLL0 group: PLL1's SSC step is
    clobbered and
    CMN_PLL0_SS_CTRL2_M0 (0x0099) is never written. Both odd, so silicon
    cannot arbitrate.

SSC is off (CMN_PLL1_SS_CTRL1_M0 = 0x0002), which is what the capture
reads and what `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` chose; the ssc_en arm differs in that one register and
is not ported.

The capture is DP and not USB3 leftovers

12 of the 71 agreeing indices hold a different value in the 6 GiB board's
SuperSpeed dump, 8 of
them config rather than training: TX_PSC_A0 and TX_PSC_A2 on lanes 0/1,
XCVR_DIAG_HSCLK_SEL on lanes 0/1, XCVR_DIAG_BIDI_CTRL on lane 1, and
0xC010 (0x51D9 here against 0x5100 under USB3). The wrapper writes are
confirmed from the link window of the same capture: LINK_CTRL2 =
0x00030010 (both IDDQ bits clear) and LINK_CTRL1 = 0x01100001 with the
link up.

What this deliberately does NOT do

The DP reset bracket (combo0_dp_phy_reset, which the vendor runs before
and after the rate write) and the swing/pre-emphasis tables
(combo0_dp_set_sw_pre) are not ported. So this cannot complete a DP link
on its own, and phy_configure() refuses set_voltages rather than
half-configuring. Two further patches, each against silicon.

phy_ops.configure also refuses unless the consumer has claimed the PHY for
DP. phy_set_mode_ext() records the mode even for a driver with no
.set_mode (drivers/phy/phy-core.c), and dwc3 sets it to a USB mode, so
that check is what stops a stray phy_configure() from writing this table
over a live SuperSpeed link.

Honest limits: a linked board's end state says what a working DP PHY looks
like, not which write is the gate. 81 of 156 indices are unwitnessed by
this method, 49 of them only because the capture used the wrong access
width.

Compile-tested against 6.18.36, W=1, clean, both with and without `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-register-a-typec-mux`
applied. HW-pending, single observable: with the reset bracket and swing
patches in place, a sink on Type-C J4 must train 4 lanes at 2.7 Gbps and
the PMA must read back the 71 confirmed values above.

Based on drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c from radxa/allwinner-bsp
(commit b36af3872d4e, "aiot-linux-v1.4.6 release"; the file is unchanged on
the branches cubie-aiot-v1.4.6 and cubie-aiot-v1.4.8), GPL-2.0,
Copyright(c) 2020 - 2023 Allwinner Technology Co.,Ltd. and
Copyright (C) 2024 Allwinner Electronics Co., Ltd. The rate table is ported
from its combo0_dp_set_rate(), the lane-count write from combo0_dp_set_lanes():
https://github.com/radxa/allwinner-bsp/blob/b36af3872d4e6e91da6e929f4ccf4822b3ea9d66/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c
The register names are those of drivers/phy/cadence/phy-cadence-torrent.c
(Linux 6.18, GPL-2.0-only, Copyright 2018 Cadence Design Systems, Inc.).

Assisted-by: Claude

