+ * "cannot say" is 81 and splits three ways, each meaning something different:
+ *
+ *   u  49 indices are ODD, and an odd index sits at byte offset 2 mod 4. Both
+ *      captures were taken with 32-bit reads on a 4-byte stride, so those
+ *      addresses were never sampled at all -- and a 32-bit read of this PMA
+ *      returns the 16-bit register mirrored into both halves rather than a
+ *      packed pair (0x0090 = 0x0180 and 0x0091 = 0x9DBA read back as
+ *      0x01800180, not 0x9DBA0180), so the odd half cannot be recovered from
+ *      the even read either. This is a limit of the capture method, not of the
+ *      silicon: a 16-bit read at 2-byte stride would settle all 49.
+ *   z  28 indices are written as zero and read as zero. A zero read-back cannot
+ *      tell a successful write from an untouched register.
+ *   x   4 indices are written non-zero and read zero: RX_SDCAL0/1_INIT_TMR on
+ *      RX lanes 0 and 3. They read zero on BOTH boards and in BOTH personalities
+ *      (the 6 GiB board's USB3 dump reads the same), while lanes 1 and 2 hold 0x001A in
+ *      both. Lanes 0 and 3 are the pairs the receptacle wires to SSTX
+ *, so the likely reading is that the RX CDB
+ *      is not populated there -- unproven either way, and the write is kept
+ *      because that is what the linked board ran.
+ *
+ * t  The four "silicon differs" are TX_TXCC_MGNFS_MULT_000 on all four lanes,
+ *    and the difference is not a wrong value -- it is link training. The vendor
+ *    rewrites that register (and TX_TXCC_CTRL, DRV_DIAG_TX_DRV,
+ *    TX_TXCC_CPOST_MULT_00) from combo0_dp_set_sw_pre() at every swing/pre-
+ *    emphasis step, and the live quartet 0x0010/0x000E/0x000E/0x0010 is verbatim
+ *    the vendor's own dp_lv_table_2g7[sw=2][pre=0] MGNFS row
+ *    (sunxi-cadence-combophy.c:556-559). At that same row TXDRV is 0x0003 and
+ *    CPOST is 0x0000, which is why the other three agree: training happened to
+ *    land on the config value. All 16 are marked "t" -- they are not config
+ *    constants and this table is not their authority. Same class as the four
+ *    `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` named (0x01C1, 0xC011, 0xE003, 0xE005); note that all four of those
+ *    are odd indices, so what `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`'s header reports for them ("silicon reads
+ *    0x001B" for 0xE003) is the mirrored value of the even neighbour 0xE002 and
+ *    not a reading of 0xE003 at all. They stay in the table as the vendor writes
+ *    them, and stay unproven.
+ *
+ * The capture is of the DP personality and not of USB3 leftovers: 12 of the 71
+ * agreeing indices hold a different value in the 6 GiB board's SuperSpeed dump, 8 of them
+ * config rather than training -- TX_PSC_A0/A2 and XCVR_DIAG_HSCLK_SEL on lanes
+ * 0/1, XCVR_DIAG_BIDI_CTRL lane 1, and 0xC010 (0x51D9 here against 0x5100 under
+ * USB3, the value the task of picking a rate turns on).
+ *
+ * Orientation: the capture is REVERSE and this table is written for none. That
+ * is sound rather than lucky -- every lane-block entry here is written to all
+ * four lanes with the same value, so the ^0x600 mirror `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` uses maps the table
+ * onto itself. Checked mechanically: of 87 lane indices the mirror moves exactly
+ * one, RX_PSC_A3 lane 0 <-> lane 3, and only because of the vendor typo below.
+ * So no mirror is applied, and a REVERSE capture is a valid witness for it.
+ *
+ * Three vendor copy-paste faults are reproduced rather than repaired, because
+ * the only evidence we have is a board that trained a 2.7 Gbps link running
+ * exactly this sequence, typos included. Naming them is the point:
+ *   - the group commented TX_PSC_A3 writes TX_PSC_A2 a second time, so
+ *     TX_PSC_A3 (0x4103 + ln*0x200) is never written;
